// Expected: a1 at two sessions, a0 at one.
//
// Pins injective agreement (query.rs). At --sessions 2 the attacker swaps
// session 2's honestly signed pair (na#2, sg#2) into session 1's Bob:
// SIGNVERIF passes, because sk is long-term and shared, so both sessions'
// signatures verify under the same key. Alice honestly produced that pair, so
// nothing here is forged — but she sent it once and Bob now accepts it twice,
// which is exactly what injective agreement forbids.
//
// At one session there is no sibling run to draw the pair from, so the query
// holds: injectivity needs two runs before it can be violated at all. That
// makes the one-session verdict the place where forgery proper stays pinned —
// sk never leaks, and mixed pairs (na#2 with session 1's sg) fail SIGNVERIF
// and halt Bob.
//
// The trace must narrate this as a replay and not as a substitution; see
// a_replayed_value_is_narrated_as_a_replay_and_not_as_a_forgery.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private sk
	pk = PUBKEY(sk)
	generates na
	sg = SIGN(sk, na)
]

Alice -> Bob: [pk], na, sg

principal Bob[
	_ = SIGNVERIF(pk, na, sg)?
	h = HASH(na)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: na
]
